WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.400
So today we're going
to talk about data.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.880
And where we are is
that you know how

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.720
to train a model, given data.

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:17.880
Last time we saw evaluations,
so we know what a good model is.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.340
And for the next two
lectures, this week

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.760
we're going to focus on what
data should we train on.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.480
And I want to argue that data
is the most important thing

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.880
to get right in language models.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.720
And one justification
for this is

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.020
if you look at what
companies actually disclose--

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.120
so here is the Llama 3 paper.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.540
They have full transparency
into the architecture--

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.440
well of course, because
it's open-weight model--

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:47.600
they even tell you about
their training procedures,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.120
but they don't say
anything about their data.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.400
So they just say, we train
for a variety of data sources

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.560
and do some stuff.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.200
And there's good reasons
for this secrecy.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:07.360
One is that data is your
competitive secret sauce.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.260
Competitive dynamics.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.160
You don't want your competitors
to know what you're doing.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.040
And the second
thing, which we'll

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.258
talk a little bit more about,
is copyright liability.

00:01:16.258 --> 00:01:18.800
You don't want to get sued if
you tell people you're training

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.600
on certain types of data.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.480
So data is a long topic
in machine learning.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.520
And before foundation
models, data work

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:33.880
meant you annotate-- label
data for supervised learning.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.380
Nowadays there's less
manual annotation--

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.560
at least in pre-training--
but there's still

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.600
a lot of curation and cleaning
work that needs to be done.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.500
And so fundamentally, this
hasn't really changed.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:48.960
Data has always
been a bottleneck,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.120
and always will be a
bottleneck because it's

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.960
in some sense a
long-tail problem,

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.620
and that scales
with human effort.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.370
So if you have a lot of people
trying to work on the problem,

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.170
well, there's only
so many people

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.670
that can work on
architectures and systems.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:06.676
But data, well, that's--
especially if you're training

00:02:06.676 --> 00:02:09.009
a foundation model that's
trying to do all these things,

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:10.910
you can [? paralyze ?]
that effort easily.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:15.130
So that's why these data teams
and these model developers

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:18.410
are actually quite big.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:23.850
So data comes in at different
stages of the pipeline.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.530
Today we're focusing
mostly on pre-training.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.350
So pre-training,
you take raw data--

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.370
these are documents
from the web.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.570
Then you move into
mid-training where

00:02:32.570 --> 00:02:34.650
you train on more
high quality data

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.010
to enhance certain capabilities,
and give long context, things

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:38.588
like that.

00:02:38.588 --> 00:02:40.130
And then finally
post training, where

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:43.410
you're training on things
like chat transcripts,

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:45.910
or if you're doing
reinforcement learning,

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:48.210
you get some environments
that you're training on.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.970
This becomes more task specific.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.730
In practice the lines
are a bit blurry,

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:59.770
and there could be
more than three stages.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.810
But this is a basic template.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.270
The trend is that
we go from training

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:06.770
on large amounts
of low quality data

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:10.770
to smaller amounts
of high quality data.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:13.610
So as a result of this process--

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.050
you'll see in the literature
when we talk about the base

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:19.490
model, This usually means after
pre-training and mid training,

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:24.330
instruct models or chat
models, or post-training.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.490
But of course, the
lines are becoming

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.770
blurry enough that what
is the base model anymore

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.790
is really unclear.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:36.070
And more recently, the largest
models, there's no base model.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:39.510
There's just Qwen3.5-397B-A17B,
and that's it.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:44.010
You don't see the
intermediate checkpoints.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:48.550
So for models that are open
source like OLMo from AI2,

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.590
you can see everything
that's happening.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:54.690
And so, we'll study
some of these models.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.570
So for example, in pre-training,
there's a bunch of sources which

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.050
we'll talk about what
these mean, but--

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:06.050
web pages, academic papers,
math pages, proofs, and so on.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:09.510
There's mid-training--
higher quality web data,

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:14.410
instruction data, bunch of
synthetic data often goes here.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:19.990
And then finally post-training,
there is a bunch of chat logs.

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:21.410
There are more
math and reasoning,

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:27.170
and coding, you do safety
at this point as well.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:30.010
So what are all these datasets?

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:33.090
How do you choose them, and
how do you process them?

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:36.730
So that's the main question.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:40.510
So in the spirit of the class,
we're going to talk about data.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:42.510
I'm going to go
start from scratch.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:45.410
So where does data come from?

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:50.210
So one might hear
in the hallway,

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:53.300
oh, language models are
trained on the entire internet.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:56.540
So first of all, this doesn't
really quite type-check.

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.380
Because for that to
be true, it would

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.300
have to be an agent that--
like an RL agent that goes

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:02.920
on the internet and does stuff.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.620
But that's not actually
how pre-training works.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:09.700
Slightly more accurately, it's
trained on the public world

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:14.180
wide web, but this is not
quite accurate for reasons

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.060
I'll explain.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:21.420
OK, so first of all, the web is
actually a bunch of live servers

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.040
that just exist in the world,
and you can connect to.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:27.740
So take any website-- you
can go and download the page.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:31.420
Or, you can basically send a
request and get back a response.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:34.100
So you can't actually
train on these live servers

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:36.620
unless you're
training an RL agent.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:40.060
So typically what you do
is you have a crawler,

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:43.620
or someone builds a crawler,
not necessarily you.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:47.280
The crawler needs to discover
what web pages are out there,

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.700
starting with the seed set, and
it downloads the discovered web

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:52.480
pages as it is crawling.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.660
But still, can't
just run a crawl

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:01.980
and download all the web
pages on the internet,

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:05.260
and there's a few
reasons for this.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:09.480
One is that a lot of the web
content is actually dynamic.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:12.460
So especially these days,
a lot of sites are apps.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:15.220
The URL isn't even as full
[? of ?] specification

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:20.180
of the content, it's just an
app that you interact with,

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:23.060
and you need to often click
buttons or submit forms

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:25.140
to access content.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.520
For example, if you're
on Discord or something,

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:32.900
it's not like you can just crawl
Discord and get all the content

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:34.860
on Discord.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.060
So that's one kind of
thing, and a lot of content

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:42.580
is actually known as in the
deep web, which is not just

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:46.860
doing web page
crawling and following

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.990
hyperlinks, which is a very
of traditional model of web

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:52.550
crawling.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:56.450
The second thing is
that authentication.

00:06:56.450 --> 00:07:00.510
So some pages need a login,
an account, and generally

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:03.550
you have to pay.

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:08.150
For example, there's Facebook,
X, LinkedIn, New York Times.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:10.030
There's actually huge
amounts of content

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:12.690
that are actually locked up
behind these walled gardens.

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.950
So these are technically on
the internet or on the web,

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:21.350
but you can't just build a
crawler and go crawl Facebook.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:23.383
I guess if you're
Facebook, you can crawl--

00:07:23.383 --> 00:07:25.550
you don't need to crawl
Facebook, you have the data,

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:26.633
and you can train a model.

00:07:26.633 --> 00:07:30.690
Or if you're X AI, you can train
on X. But if you're anyone else,

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:34.830
you can't actually
access this content.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:38.610
OK, suppose you don't have
the authentication issue.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:43.210
There's still potential
restrictions here.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:46.350
So there's something
called robots.txt,

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:49.070
and this is a file
that is usually

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:54.290
placed at the root level of a
directory, such as nytimes.com,

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:55.910
and it basically
tells you what you're

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:57.670
allowed to crawl and whatnot.

00:07:57.670 --> 00:08:00.250
So these things are-- a lot
of things are disallowed,

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:06.030
such as OAI-SearchBot, or
PerplexityBot, QuoraBot.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:12.030
I think basically, ChatGPT-User,
ClaudeBot, and all these things.

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:17.350
OK, so this is not a tech--

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:19.467
this is not a legal
restriction, this

00:08:19.467 --> 00:08:21.550
is just like, you're
supposed to be a good citizen

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:23.490
and look at robots.txt.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.550
And if your name
is on that list,

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:26.970
then you should not crawl it.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:31.150
That's basically the--
not even a contract,

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:36.470
that is the thing
you're supposed to do.

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:40.630
A lot of websites now use
something like Cloudflare

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:43.470
to detect and
block bot activity.

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:45.290
So sometimes you've
gone to a website,

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:46.930
and it gives you a CAPTCHA.

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:49.230
That's because
somehow, some algorithm

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.150
has detected that
you might be a bot,

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.110
and then it's making
sure you're human.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:56.942
And if you're actually a
bot, then, well, you can't--

00:08:56.942 --> 00:08:58.650
I mean, you could try
to get around this,

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.950
but generally this is
an obstacle in your way.

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:06.590
A website might block certain
IP addresses or countries,

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:08.370
and they might have rate limits.

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:09.750
So there's technical
restrictions

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:15.030
on what you can actually
crawl, and then there's

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:17.350
also legal restrictions.

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:22.070
So websites have
terms of service

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:23.510
that say, if you
go to a website,

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.830
you have to obey this
contract to use the website.

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:30.090
And the terms of service often
will say, if you're a bot,

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:30.910
go away.

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:34.230
You cannot use this, the content
of the site for AI training,

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:37.030
or whatever it might be.

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:42.870
And also, even if terms of
service don't say anything,

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:47.760
the content of the website
might have a license,

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.460
or you might not have
a license to train.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:52.760
And we'll talk more
about this in a bit.

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:54.400
So there's legal
restrictions on what

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:57.160
you are allowed to train on.

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:00.360
These things are also
evolving over time.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:03.400
So there's a nice
paper by Shane Longpre

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:07.120
called Consent in
Crisis, and what

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:09.380
they did is they examined
the restrictions--

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:11.760
both the technical
restrictions, robots.txt,

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.800
and the legal restrictions,
the term of service,

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.100
for various URLs
in common datasets,

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.240
which we'll talk about later.

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.600
And the conclusion was
that the restrictions

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:22.900
have increased over time.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:28.160
So here's a graphic that shows--
the top one shows robots.txt.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:29.920
And if you just
look at this red,

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.840
that's the full restrictions.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:39.480
And up until 2023, everything
was fairly constant.

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:43.680
And then up until mid
2023, all of a sudden

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:46.560
you see the amount of
the fraction of websites

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:51.920
that have full restrictions on
it has grown to almost 50% here.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:11:00.000
And for terms, there's a similar
trend where I guess in 2016,

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.460
no one really put any
terms on their pages.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:07.920
And now most pages put some
terms, and most of the terms

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:12.800
say you can't use this for AI.

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:18.560
So even though it was possible
to crawl the internet back in,

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:22.000
let's say 2020, the
internet actually,

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:26.260
at least what you can legally
crawl, is actually much smaller.

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:34.060
So of course, these
are guidelines.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:36.360
And sometimes,
either intentionally

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:40.140
or not intentionally, you
could violate these things.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:44.640
So this is-- sometimes you get
into these situations where

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:47.820
there's this guy who--

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:48.960
what was this?

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.320
I forget the name of this site--

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:54.480
was complaining about
Anthropic crawling them,

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.080
hitting their servers
a million times

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:59.960
in 24 hours, which was not good.

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.100
And then Read the Docs
was also getting hammered.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:04.280
So there was a period where,
which I think probably

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:07.560
has hopefully been corrected
by now, that crawlers

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:12.420
were hammering websites.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:16.840
And there the-- here,
this is interesting,

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:19.560
is like even before talking
about copyright issues,

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:24.120
there's, I think, problems
with crawling either violating

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:31.600
terms of service, or
robots.txt, or just low up-down,

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:34.280
generating a lot of server
load, which this costs money

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:37.080
for the person hosting it
and also degrades service

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:39.520
for everyone else
trying to use it.

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:42.885
So there are issues
with crawling.

00:12:42.885 --> 00:12:44.510
And then, of course,
there's copyright,

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:46.830
which we'll talk about soon.

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:50.970
That's a whole other
complex can of worms.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:55.090
And then finally, there's these
things called shadow libraries,

00:12:55.090 --> 00:12:58.010
which are also part of the web.

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:03.010
So examples are LibGen,
or Anna's Archive.

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:07.150
And these completely disregard
copyright and bypass paywalls.

00:13:07.150 --> 00:13:11.690
So someone has collected
a ton of data-- usually

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:14.190
books and articles that
are all copyrighted,

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:16.130
and people have
to pay for these--

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:20.530
and have made them
available for free.

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:23.910
And there have been a lot of
takedown orders, lawsuits,

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:26.410
and these are blocked,
but usually these

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:30.090
are circumvented
because they just make

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:32.490
servers in other countries.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:37.010
And the people doing this
argue that this is making free

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:39.850
what's freely available,
what should have been freed,

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:42.290
but generally from
a legal perspective,

00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:45.270
this is piracy and
copyright infringement.

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:47.390
And we'll come back
to this point later.

00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:52.530
So there are a lot of books
and papers on these shadow

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:54.850
libraries.

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:57.850
So the summary so
far, the internet

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:03.010
is a huge and messy
and scary place,

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:06.850
and you can't actually
get all of it.

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:08.510
There are technical
restrictions,

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:12.730
there's legal restrictions
on what data one can access.

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:16.070
So now next time someone says,
I trained on the internet,

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:20.090
you can tell them all
these things you learned.

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:28.450
So now let's go to the second
part of what data can we use,

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:31.370
and this has to
do with copyright.

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:35.610
So suppose you were able to
obey the terms of service,

00:14:35.610 --> 00:14:38.610
and you are really
a good citizen,

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:41.620
and you obey the rate limits.

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:43.380
Now, there's still a
question of are you

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:46.500
allowed to train on this data?

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:50.580
And this is a
ongoing question that

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:53.260
has not been fully
resolved, but there

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:57.020
has been a bunch of
developments in the last year.

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:00.260
So the legal context for
thinking about this question

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:03.260
falls into intellectual
property law,

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:08.500
and this is a system
that is trying

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:11.360
to incentivize the creation
of intellectual goods.

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:13.420
So I think it's--

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:15.780
remember, the
spirit of the law is

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:20.800
to not just technically
say no to everything,

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:24.860
but to incentivize the
creation of intellectual goods.

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:27.620
And there's many forms
of intellectual property

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:29.180
that are covered.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:33.660
There's copyrights, patents,
trademarks and secrets-- sorry,

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:36.620
trademarks, and trade secrets.

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:41.140
The thing that is most relevant
for language model data

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:43.220
is copyright law.

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:46.980
So copyright law has a fairly
long history going back

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:49.820
to the 1700s in England.

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:53.460
And so 1709 was the first time
that governments and courts

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:56.340
started actually
regulating copyright

00:15:56.340 --> 00:16:01.740
for this goal of incentivizing
innovation and creation.

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:05.580
And in the US, more recently,
the Copyright Act of 1976

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:10.020
is what I think defines a
lot of modern copyright.

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:13.620
So copyright protection
applies to original works

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:18.580
of authorship fixed in any
tangible medium of expression,

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:19.900
yada, yada, yada.

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:23.920
So a few notes.

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:26.820
So not everything
is copyrightable.

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:31.220
In particular, collections
are not copyrightable.

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:36.900
So for example, if you
have a telephone directory,

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:41.540
unless there is some creativity
in how you arrange things,

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:45.220
this is not really
copyrightable.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:48.460
And furthermore, copyright
applies to the expression, not

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:49.860
the idea.

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:53.180
So you can't copyright an
idea, like you can't copyright

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:55.360
the quick-sort algorithm.

00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:57.380
You can copyright
the implementation

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:01.740
of a quick-sort algorithm, but
not the quicksort algorithm.

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:04.339
So one thing that
happened in 1976

00:17:04.339 --> 00:17:10.359
is that the barrier to getting
a copyright was relaxed a lot.

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:11.640
So more things are copyright.

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.300
So it used to be that you
had to publish something

00:17:14.300 --> 00:17:15.760
to have something copyright.

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:19.260
Now things just
have to be fixed.

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:22.780
And what that means is that
registration of any sort

00:17:22.780 --> 00:17:26.388
is not required for
copyright protection.

00:17:26.388 --> 00:17:27.680
This is in contrast to patents.

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:31.060
Patents you have to go pay a
lot of money to get a patent,

00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:32.980
and you have to file a patent.

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:36.190
But the threshold for
copyright is very low.

00:17:36.190 --> 00:17:38.470
For example, you put
something on your website,

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:40.550
it's copyrighted.

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:42.670
That's it.

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:45.990
Now if you want to
sue someone, you

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:50.670
have to go get it registered,
but it only costs $65.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:53.310
Which is much smaller
than the lawyer fees

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:56.190
that you'll probably
pay, so it's really

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:59.670
still a very low bar.

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:01.350
So one thing to
know about copyright

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:07.330
is that it lasts 75 years, and
then the copyright expires.

00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:11.230
And whatever that content gets
released into the public domain,

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:13.750
and this is now--

00:18:13.750 --> 00:18:15.550
everyone can use it freely.

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:21.070
So a lot of people who
have lived in the past,

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:23.250
all their great works
are in the public domain,

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:24.990
which is great for everyone.

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:28.950
So the rationale here is that
you're incentivizing innovation.

00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:33.870
So when an artist or a
creator creates something,

00:18:33.870 --> 00:18:36.470
you want to protect their--

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:38.590
you don't want someone
else to just take it,

00:18:38.590 --> 00:18:40.010
so you protect them for a while.

00:18:40.010 --> 00:18:43.790
But after 75 years, presumably,
it's not worth protecting,

00:18:43.790 --> 00:18:45.650
or they're passed
away, or something.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:50.830
So it doesn't make sense to
keep things copyright anymore.

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:57.670
So basically, everything on
the internet is copyrighted.

00:18:57.670 --> 00:19:00.150
So you're saying, wait a minute.

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:03.170
So that means is everything--
if I train on anything,

00:19:03.170 --> 00:19:04.730
that's a copyright violation?

00:19:04.730 --> 00:19:07.030
Well, not necessarily.

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:09.350
So everything is
copyrighted, but you

00:19:09.350 --> 00:19:10.850
can use copyrighted works.

00:19:10.850 --> 00:19:16.190
And the way you do it is A, you
either get a license for it,

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:20.750
or B, you appeal to
the fair use clause.

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:25.470
So a license from
contract law is something

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:28.050
that a licensor
grants to a licensee.

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:30.230
It basically says, don't sue me.

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:38.790
You can use this work in the
ways that the license permits.

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:44.790
So there's a really nice
license called Creative Commons,

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:48.150
which allows something
to act like it

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:50.150
was in the public domain.

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:52.570
It enables free distribution
of copyrighted work.

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:58.690
So examples include Wikipedia,
Open CourseWare, Khan Academy,

00:19:58.690 --> 00:20:00.390
all these things.

00:20:00.390 --> 00:20:04.510
It was created in 2001
to essentially bridge

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:06.330
public domain and
existing copyright,

00:20:06.330 --> 00:20:09.790
because otherwise you
have to wait 75 years.

00:20:09.790 --> 00:20:13.930
But what if the creator says, I
actually want people to use it.

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:16.810
They can now have a means,
a legal means of saying,

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:19.250
I put a Creative
Commons license on it.

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:22.430
It's going to act like
it's in the public domain,

00:20:22.430 --> 00:20:27.430
and everyone can use it.

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:29.590
So, so far, things
in the public domain

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:34.240
and things that have Creative
Commons licenses are permissive.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:37.040
You can use it.

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:42.000
But there are many
other times where

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.860
you don't have-- it's not
a Creative Commons license,

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:47.540
it's not in the public domain,
you have to get a license.

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:51.960
So if you have money,
you can pay someone

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:53.140
to give you a license.

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:55.920
So there's a bunch of deals
between model developers

00:20:55.920 --> 00:21:01.040
and content, I guess,
platforms, that

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:04.600
allow the model developers to
license the data for training

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:06.360
foundation models.

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:07.860
OK, so that's the first thing.

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:09.960
You can get a license--
either Creative Commons,

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:12.800
or you can pay
someone for a license.

00:21:12.800 --> 00:21:14.820
Now if you don't
want to pay, you

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:21.320
can appeal to fair use, which
is a more complicated matter.

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:24.600
So this is section 107
of the Copyright Act.

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:30.080
And there are-- basically fair
use says, I can use this anyway,

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:33.480
even if I don't have a license,
and these four factors determine

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:36.240
whether fair use applies.

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:40.300
So the first one is the purpose
or character of the use.

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:42.120
What are you trying
to do with it?

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:45.980
And none of these
are hard rules.

00:21:45.980 --> 00:21:48.200
They're just
tendencies which have

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:51.040
to be weighed in court
if it comes to that.

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:53.120
So, for example, if you're
trying to do something

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:55.282
for education, it
is more likely to be

00:21:55.282 --> 00:21:57.240
fair use than if you're
trying to sell and make

00:21:57.240 --> 00:21:58.980
money off of it.

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:01.800
If you're trying to-- if
you transform something,

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:03.520
then that's going
to be more favored

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:07.800
than if you just literally
host the identical copy.

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:10.400
The nature of the
actual copyrighted work

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:12.080
is also important.

00:22:12.080 --> 00:22:14.240
So things that are
more factual are

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:17.640
more likely to be fair
use than fictional works.

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:21.840
Because if you write a
summary of World War--

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:25.680
a page about facts
about World War II,

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:35.120
there's less protection over
my really creative poem.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:39.820
And then the amount of
portion of the original work.

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:43.545
So using a snippet is
favored over the whole work.

00:22:43.545 --> 00:22:44.920
So if you just
take a little bit,

00:22:44.920 --> 00:22:46.740
that's not as bad as
taking everything.

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.480
So that's fairly
straightforward.

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:53.760
And the final thing is related
to original motivation.

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:54.960
It was like, why are you--

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:58.440
why copyright at all
is to protect and align

00:22:58.440 --> 00:23:00.080
the economic incentives.

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:03.720
So the effect that
what the use has

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:06.440
on the market for
the original work.

00:23:06.440 --> 00:23:12.400
So if you take an author's work
and you provide an alternative,

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:16.720
and that decreases the amount
that the original author could

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:20.260
monetize their work, that
is seen as potentially bad.

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:24.480
But if you're transforming it in
a way and going to a new market,

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:26.490
or doing something else
with it, then that's

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:30.250
going to be treated
more favorably.

00:23:30.250 --> 00:23:32.710
So here are some
examples of fair use.

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:35.350
You can watch a movie and
write a summary of it.

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:40.290
You can re-implement an
idea, like an algorithm--

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:42.410
not copy the code.

00:23:42.410 --> 00:23:48.810
And then there was this famous
lawsuit between Authors Guild

00:23:48.810 --> 00:23:50.650
and Google.

00:23:50.650 --> 00:23:52.610
So if you go to
Google Books, you

00:23:52.610 --> 00:23:57.490
can see snippets of various
books which are copyrighted.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:24:04.610
So there was a huge
deal about whether this

00:24:04.610 --> 00:24:08.570
was deemed fair use, because
you are redistributing

00:24:08.570 --> 00:24:14.890
other copyrighted
work, and eventually--

00:24:14.890 --> 00:24:16.870
eventually meaning
after 11 years--

00:24:16.870 --> 00:24:19.890
this lawsuit was settled
in favor of Google,

00:24:19.890 --> 00:24:23.010
which is why this thing exists.

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:26.530
And this has set some
precedent for thinking about

00:24:26.530 --> 00:24:33.290
whether language model
training is fair use.

00:24:33.290 --> 00:24:36.170
One thing to note is
that copyright is not

00:24:36.170 --> 00:24:39.050
about verbatim memorization.

00:24:39.050 --> 00:24:41.870
So this might be, I
think, for an ML audience,

00:24:41.870 --> 00:24:44.890
this might be a little bit
new because a lot of papers

00:24:44.890 --> 00:24:47.370
are focused on
verbatim memorization.

00:24:47.370 --> 00:24:51.210
That's one way you can violate
copyright, but not the only one.

00:24:51.210 --> 00:24:55.490
So for example, plots and
characters can be copyrightable.

00:24:55.490 --> 00:24:57.090
So you can copyright
Harry Potter

00:24:57.090 --> 00:25:02.770
the character, not any
particular book, or anything.

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:04.870
But there are some exceptions.

00:25:04.870 --> 00:25:08.090
For example, if you're
parodying something--

00:25:08.090 --> 00:25:11.390
and so you're creating something
that looks like a derivative,

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:13.270
but as long as you're
making fun of it,

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:16.570
it's actually more
likely to be fair use.

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:18.450
So, yeah.

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:20.710
So a lot of copyright
is about semantics.

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:23.170
It's definitely not
about N-gram overlap,

00:25:23.170 --> 00:25:27.340
and also about the economics.

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:30.660
So what is the implication
for language models?

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:36.340
So first off, copying data.

00:25:36.340 --> 00:25:40.660
Even if you're not training,
even the mere fact of copying--

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:43.180
which is in the word copyright--

00:25:43.180 --> 00:25:45.840
is potentially a
violation already,

00:25:45.840 --> 00:25:47.720
even if you don't
do anything with it.

00:25:50.500 --> 00:25:53.340
Training a model--

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:58.640
I guess this is not really a--
let's see, how do I take that?

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:03.540
This is not necessarily
a fact, but intuitively

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:05.920
has a transformative flavor.

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:07.460
Because it's certainly not--

00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:09.900
it certainly seems
different than just

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:12.580
rehosting another work.

00:26:12.580 --> 00:26:17.420
It's doing something
transformative.

00:26:17.420 --> 00:26:21.740
And in some sense,
the models are really

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:25.080
trained on this data
as a means to an end.

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:26.960
You are trying to
extract the general idea,

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:29.560
learn about the world
and how it works,

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:34.940
rather than only just
the concrete expression.

00:26:34.940 --> 00:26:39.200
And the other thing is that
regardless of copyright,

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:43.400
language models can
definitely affect the market.

00:26:43.400 --> 00:26:46.180
So I think this is
also-- remember, that's

00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:49.500
the fourth item for fair use.

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:51.260
So by negatively
affecting the market,

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:54.860
you are more likely to
be ruled not fair use.

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:03.540
So fair use is a
little bit slippery.

00:27:03.540 --> 00:27:07.340
Suppose you have fair use,
or you have a license.

00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:11.260
Remember that there's still
terms of service that allow

00:27:11.260 --> 00:27:15.200
or prevent you from actually
just getting a piece of work.

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:20.460
For example, YouTube has all
these videos which are licensed,

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:24.460
but the terms of
service prohibits

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:30.100
just downloading the videos
using a bot, like scraping,

00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:31.280
which is another layer.

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:35.900
So there's multiple layers
of restrictions here.

00:27:35.900 --> 00:27:38.380
So let me talk a little
bit about where things

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:40.400
are on the legal landscape.

00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:44.080
So back in 2023, New York Times
filed suit against OpenAI,

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:47.520
saying you train on our
news articles, and look,

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:48.240
here's evidence.

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:52.540
We were able to actually prompt
ChatGPT to generate a news

00:27:52.540 --> 00:27:55.020
article almost verbatim.

00:27:55.020 --> 00:27:57.780
I don't think this
is still pending.

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:02.780
There's a case against Anthropic
where the allegation was

00:28:02.780 --> 00:28:06.100
you pirated millions of books,
and you trained on these books

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:08.700
to make Claude.

00:28:08.700 --> 00:28:14.220
Just last year, this
was a landmark ruling

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:17.900
that said, actually, this
particular instance of training

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:20.670
is fair use.

00:28:20.670 --> 00:28:23.350
However, Anthropic,
you're still in trouble

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:29.310
because you pirated all these
books, and that is a no-no.

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:33.230
So notice that this has nothing
to do with training, just

00:28:33.230 --> 00:28:35.650
the mere fact of pirating--

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:38.950
I mean, which is
not a new thing--

00:28:38.950 --> 00:28:43.110
is illegal.

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:45.250
Anthropic actually,
interestingly,

00:28:45.250 --> 00:28:48.790
had bought and
scanned all the books,

00:28:48.790 --> 00:28:50.170
which is actually fair use.

00:28:50.170 --> 00:28:52.430
The Court said you're
allowed to scan.

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:54.690
You buy a bunch of books,
rip off the bindings,

00:28:54.690 --> 00:28:56.730
scan it, and digitize
it for your own use.

00:28:56.730 --> 00:28:58.030
That's fair use.

00:28:58.030 --> 00:29:02.610
But that doesn't absolve
you of your sin of pirating.

00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:06.270
So you can't pirate and then
buy it and say, actually, never

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:10.030
mind what I just did first.

00:29:10.030 --> 00:29:12.750
So the outcome is that
Anthropic paid 1.5 billion

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:14.330
to settle the authors.

00:29:14.330 --> 00:29:18.510
So this is about $3,000 a book.

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:22.750
So there's also a
lawsuit against Meta.

00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:25.830
The allegation was that
you trained on our books--

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:29.170
sorry-- and this was,
as we'll see later,

00:29:29.170 --> 00:29:31.710
actually revealed
in the Llama paper.

00:29:31.710 --> 00:29:36.250
And the judgment which came
right after Anthropic was, yes,

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:39.270
training is fair use.

00:29:39.270 --> 00:29:41.650
And then they also
torrented some books,

00:29:41.650 --> 00:29:42.890
so that's still pending.

00:29:42.890 --> 00:29:47.050
So that's probably--
if precedent holds,

00:29:47.050 --> 00:29:52.350
this is also not going
to be good for Meta.

00:29:52.350 --> 00:30:00.430
So the summary so far is
training, so to speak,

00:30:00.430 --> 00:30:03.390
has been deemed of
fair use, or at least

00:30:03.390 --> 00:30:07.950
has been not deemed
not fair use.

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:10.610
The rulings have
so far been narrow.

00:30:10.610 --> 00:30:13.550
It's not to say that any
training on any copyrighted

00:30:13.550 --> 00:30:19.030
content is fair use, but
in these cases, it's fine.

00:30:19.030 --> 00:30:21.590
Pirating books is
clearly illegal.

00:30:21.590 --> 00:30:23.190
I guess we already knew that.

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:28.250
But this is still a very
active and evolving area.

00:30:31.070 --> 00:30:36.510
Any questions about
the sources of data?

00:30:36.510 --> 00:30:38.570
I think, before we get
into the actual datasets,

00:30:38.570 --> 00:30:43.830
I just want to set the
stage with this more

00:30:43.830 --> 00:30:46.530
general social context.

00:30:51.690 --> 00:30:52.190
Yeah?

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:55.530
[INAUDIBLE]

00:30:56.030 --> 00:30:59.430
The question is, what do I think
of voice data and ElevenLabs?

00:30:59.430 --> 00:31:04.810
I don't know too much about
that, so we can talk offline.

00:31:04.810 --> 00:31:05.310
Yeah?

00:31:05.310 --> 00:31:07.510
[INAUDIBLE]

00:31:07.510 --> 00:31:11.790
--because I guess if I own some
asset, it's reasonable for me

00:31:11.790 --> 00:31:14.190
to be like [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:17.180
Am I allowed to-- usually
it's Creative Commons,

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:21.960
but can I update my license
later on to block certain usage?

00:31:21.960 --> 00:31:24.320
And so, what is the
role of time playing

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:29.280
in this, like I then train on
the older version with more

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:32.000
a more permissive license
if the creator copied it

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:33.918
to a more restrictive?

00:31:33.918 --> 00:31:35.960
Yeah, so the question is,
what happens with time?

00:31:35.960 --> 00:31:40.220
If you have one license and then
it gets changed, what happens?

00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:42.840
So first, I'm not a lawyer.

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:46.840
But second, so this--

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:49.880
so the first license
I believe you're still

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:51.820
allowed to train on it.

00:31:51.820 --> 00:31:54.840
But what happens is
that the license usually

00:31:54.840 --> 00:31:58.227
applies to not just, let's
say, a fixed set of documents--

00:31:58.227 --> 00:31:59.060
for example, Reddit.

00:31:59.060 --> 00:32:01.800
There's constantly new
content being created,

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:03.680
and the license
being changed means

00:32:03.680 --> 00:32:07.160
that you wouldn't be able to
train on the later things.

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:17.760
OK, so let's look at
various sources of data.

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:20.880
So let's go back to crawling.

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:25.360
So most model developers
have their own crawler,

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:31.920
because they want to have full
control over what the data is.

00:32:31.920 --> 00:32:33.640
Fortunately for the
rest of us, if you

00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:37.160
don't want to build
your own crawler,

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:39.840
there's something called Common
Crawl, which has been around

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:43.640
for quite some time since 2007.

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:47.610
And every month they run a
web crawl, gets about three

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:50.000
to five billion web pages.

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:53.300
Each crawl has some overlap
with the previous crawl,

00:32:53.300 --> 00:32:56.360
but they try to diversify
and get new pages.

00:32:56.360 --> 00:33:00.680
So there's 300 billion
pages so far, which seems--

00:33:00.680 --> 00:33:01.852
this was from their website.

00:33:01.852 --> 00:33:03.560
It seems a little bit
big, because if you

00:33:03.560 --> 00:33:08.220
multiply this number by 20, you
don't quite get 300 billion.

00:33:08.220 --> 00:33:11.280
But that's what they say.

00:33:11.280 --> 00:33:14.150
So how many URLs are out there?

00:33:14.150 --> 00:33:16.070
That's really hard to estimate.

00:33:16.070 --> 00:33:21.650
So the Google search index
is at least 100 petabytes,

00:33:21.650 --> 00:33:23.130
according to them.

00:33:23.130 --> 00:33:32.350
And, each Common Crawl dump
has about 2 billion web pages.

00:33:32.350 --> 00:33:35.490
So that's about 372tb.

00:33:35.490 --> 00:33:37.150
And this is not
including images,

00:33:37.150 --> 00:33:41.170
this is just mostly
focused on the text.

00:33:41.170 --> 00:33:44.630
So crawling is conceptually
straightforward,

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:48.370
but all the gory details
are in the implementation.

00:33:48.370 --> 00:33:53.190
You start with a bunch of
URLs, and then you iterate.

00:33:53.190 --> 00:33:54.590
It's basically graph traversal.

00:33:54.590 --> 00:33:56.630
You pop a URL from a
queue, you download it,

00:33:56.630 --> 00:33:59.390
and then you look at all
the hyperlinks in that page,

00:33:59.390 --> 00:34:01.090
and then you add
them to the queue.

00:34:01.090 --> 00:34:06.410
And generally this is done in
parallel over many machines.

00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:10.130
And there's many
decisions here--

00:34:10.130 --> 00:34:16.610
which pages to download, you
have to respect robots.txt,

00:34:16.610 --> 00:34:19.210
don't overload the server.

00:34:19.210 --> 00:34:24.530
Sometimes the websites
change, and so you

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:30.489
want a policy that will download
frequently changed pages,

00:34:30.489 --> 00:34:33.929
but not spend time downloading
pages that don't change.

00:34:33.929 --> 00:34:35.830
So there's some
policy around that.

00:34:35.830 --> 00:34:38.310
And I mentioned before,
URLs are dynamic,

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:43.370
so sometimes the same URL
leads to different content

00:34:43.370 --> 00:34:46.290
depending on some
state of the browser.

00:34:46.290 --> 00:34:49.949
But also, many URLs might
lead to the same content,

00:34:49.949 --> 00:34:52.409
so there's a lot
of duplication that

00:34:52.409 --> 00:34:55.290
happens if you're not careful.

00:34:55.290 --> 00:35:00.170
In particular, mirror sites
explicitly is about duplication.

00:35:00.170 --> 00:35:02.770
So Common Crawl releases
their dumps in two formats.

00:35:02.770 --> 00:35:04.810
One is a WARC file.

00:35:04.810 --> 00:35:07.950
So this is the
raw HTTP response.

00:35:07.950 --> 00:35:10.530
So remember, HTTP
protocol is that you

00:35:10.530 --> 00:35:20.470
send a get, here's a URL, and
then out comes a HTTP response,

00:35:20.470 --> 00:35:25.170
and the WARC file
is that response.

00:35:25.170 --> 00:35:26.790
They also do some processing.

00:35:26.790 --> 00:35:29.850
They convert to a WET
file, which is necessarily

00:35:29.850 --> 00:35:32.490
a loss-y process.

00:35:32.490 --> 00:35:35.730
It turns out that this is
not necessarily the best way

00:35:35.730 --> 00:35:38.970
to use the web.

00:35:38.970 --> 00:35:42.370
The HTML to text,
there's many tools

00:35:42.370 --> 00:35:45.610
for this, including
trafilatura and resiliparse,

00:35:45.610 --> 00:35:48.870
and the way you convert
actually does matter.

00:35:48.870 --> 00:35:51.610
So this is from the
DataComp LLM paper, which

00:35:51.610 --> 00:35:53.070
we'll talk a little bit about.

00:35:53.070 --> 00:35:55.730
Data is an ablation, where
you look at the web files

00:35:55.730 --> 00:36:01.450
that Common Crawl releases
versus these other tools,

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:06.310
and it seems like trafilatura
and resiliparse are better.

00:36:08.900 --> 00:36:14.300
So one thing you can do is just
rely on general web crawls.

00:36:14.300 --> 00:36:17.600
But often, the web is
not a uniform place.

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:20.260
It's not like there's
a bunch of websites,

00:36:20.260 --> 00:36:22.920
and you just get some
random fraction of them,

00:36:22.920 --> 00:36:25.020
and that's your dataset.

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:28.540
There are specific pockets of
really interesting, high quality

00:36:28.540 --> 00:36:30.700
content, and I'll talk
about three of those--

00:36:30.700 --> 00:36:32.240
Wikipedia, GitHub, and arXiv.

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:36.980
So Wikipedia, all
of you know about.

00:36:36.980 --> 00:36:40.580
It's been around since
2001, and now there's

00:36:40.580 --> 00:36:46.220
67 million articles around
all these different languages.

00:36:46.220 --> 00:36:53.380
And Wikipedia is not all
content by any means.

00:36:53.380 --> 00:36:59.420
You can't have
original thought in it,

00:36:59.420 --> 00:37:03.780
because everything has to
be referenced and cited.

00:37:03.780 --> 00:37:06.700
So in some sense you
could argue that Wikipedia

00:37:06.700 --> 00:37:12.200
doesn't contain anything
that's not already on the web.

00:37:12.200 --> 00:37:14.820
Well actually, that's not
true, because they can also

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:18.580
cite books, which are--
obviously you can't easily get

00:37:18.580 --> 00:37:21.500
them, so, Wikipedia is so good.

00:37:21.500 --> 00:37:27.260
And also, there's articles
based on notability,

00:37:27.260 --> 00:37:36.020
so not everyone can get a
Wikipedia article about them.

00:37:36.020 --> 00:37:40.140
So anyone on the internet
can write this content.

00:37:40.140 --> 00:37:45.040
This was the radical thing about
wikis, and it's still a miracle,

00:37:45.040 --> 00:37:48.140
I think, to me that
this actually works.

00:37:48.140 --> 00:37:49.820
It just happens
that any vandalism

00:37:49.820 --> 00:37:53.820
gets reverted by administrators,
or bots, I guess, these days.

00:37:56.660 --> 00:37:59.580
There's a small number of
Wikipedians-- as any peer

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:01.460
production system,
there's a small set

00:38:01.460 --> 00:38:04.420
of people who do
most of the work.

00:38:04.420 --> 00:38:09.660
And for example, this
guy has 5 million edits.

00:38:09.660 --> 00:38:12.680
And one thing about Wikipedia
is that every few weeks,

00:38:12.680 --> 00:38:14.155
they do a periodic dump.

00:38:14.155 --> 00:38:15.780
So basically they
take all of Wikipedia

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:19.780
packaged into a nice
TAR file, and then you

00:38:19.780 --> 00:38:21.580
can just download that.

00:38:21.580 --> 00:38:24.840
So this is important because you
don't need to crawl Wikipedia.

00:38:24.840 --> 00:38:26.840
In fact, they don't want
you to crawl Wikipedia.

00:38:26.840 --> 00:38:29.382
They just want you to download
this if you're going to crawl,

00:38:29.382 --> 00:38:32.100
and this is much
better than crawling.

00:38:32.100 --> 00:38:35.540
Just a fun aside,
or good to know,

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:40.980
is that there's this paper
that says you can actually

00:38:40.980 --> 00:38:43.500
poison Wikipedia.

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:47.560
So you might think that this
vandalism gets reverted.

00:38:47.560 --> 00:38:50.340
So if you do-- attacker
tries to do something fishy,

00:38:50.340 --> 00:38:52.140
you'll just get it rolled back.

00:38:52.140 --> 00:38:58.340
But what Carlini realized
is that these periodic dumps

00:38:58.340 --> 00:39:00.820
happen at a regular cadence.

00:39:00.820 --> 00:39:04.230
And so, what you can
do is you can go in

00:39:04.230 --> 00:39:08.070
and you can just edit it
right before the dump happens.

00:39:08.070 --> 00:39:12.810
And then the dump happens, and
then the edits get rolled back,

00:39:12.810 --> 00:39:14.890
but the dump still has
the malicious content.

00:39:17.790 --> 00:39:21.490
If you're able to inject
things into web pages,

00:39:21.490 --> 00:39:23.590
that means you can cause--

00:39:23.590 --> 00:39:28.510
there's works that show that you
can cause the model to, let's

00:39:28.510 --> 00:39:32.310
say, ascribe negative sentiment
to any trigger phrase,

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:34.190
like the iPhone.

00:39:34.190 --> 00:39:39.490
So one takeaway here is that
if you consider adversaries,

00:39:39.490 --> 00:39:41.750
even so-called high
quality contents might

00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:44.910
contain bad things in them.

00:39:44.910 --> 00:39:50.310
I think this has been
fixed since then.

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:53.690
OK, so GitHub is a
good place for code,

00:39:53.690 --> 00:39:56.550
and code is important
not just for

00:39:56.550 --> 00:39:59.410
if you want coding capabilities
for your language model,

00:39:59.410 --> 00:40:03.630
but if you want
general reasoning.

00:40:03.630 --> 00:40:06.670
So GitHub all of you know.

00:40:06.670 --> 00:40:10.430
Again, it's a live service
for hosting code repositories.

00:40:10.430 --> 00:40:15.870
Been around since 2008, about
the same age as Common Crawl.

00:40:15.870 --> 00:40:22.470
It has 420 million repositories,
28 million are public.

00:40:22.470 --> 00:40:26.790
Each repository contains--
it's not a file, as you know,

00:40:26.790 --> 00:40:29.770
it's a directory with commit
history, issues, pull requests,

00:40:29.770 --> 00:40:31.470
and comments.

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:35.230
Code has generally
a lot of duplicates,

00:40:35.230 --> 00:40:42.150
and it's just because either
copying code, or forking code.

00:40:42.150 --> 00:40:46.430
And GitHub has
deemed it-- you're

00:40:46.430 --> 00:40:49.390
allowed to train on
any public repository

00:40:49.390 --> 00:40:52.430
with a permissive license.

00:40:52.430 --> 00:40:56.110
So MIT or Apache
licenses are fine.

00:40:56.110 --> 00:40:58.345
So remember, there's
two types of data

00:40:58.345 --> 00:40:59.470
when you talk about GitHub.

00:40:59.470 --> 00:41:01.880
There's the
repository data, which

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:07.160
you can just go and download
through the GitHub Git protocol.

00:41:07.160 --> 00:41:10.440
And again, you
shouldn't scrape GitHub,

00:41:10.440 --> 00:41:12.640
you should just
download the repository.

00:41:12.640 --> 00:41:16.120
And then the metadata associated
with each repository-- issues

00:41:16.120 --> 00:41:17.700
and pull requests, and so on.

00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:20.600
These are made actually via the
GitHub Archive, which gives you

00:41:20.600 --> 00:41:23.720
hourly snapshots of this
event stream, which I think

00:41:23.720 --> 00:41:25.380
is really interesting data.

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:29.000
It's basically every single
comment, or star, or action

00:41:29.000 --> 00:41:33.360
on GitHub that gets recorded.

00:41:33.360 --> 00:41:36.720
There's this software
heritage foundation

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:41.440
that is focused on the
repository, not the metadata,

00:41:41.440 --> 00:41:44.218
and they aggregate GitHub.

00:41:44.218 --> 00:41:46.260
GitHub is not the only
place where code shows up.

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:50.540
There's also smaller entities
like GitLab, Bitbucket,

00:41:50.540 --> 00:41:51.360
and so on.

00:41:51.360 --> 00:41:56.740
And they aggregate all
that, those repositories

00:41:56.740 --> 00:42:01.280
So arXiv, just very briefly--
everyone knows arXiv.

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:04.360
This has been around as
a site that allows people

00:42:04.360 --> 00:42:06.340
to share papers since 1991.

00:42:06.340 --> 00:42:12.560
Started with physics, and now
has a lot of other areas in it.

00:42:12.560 --> 00:42:15.760
Each of the three
million submissions

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:21.640
has the metadata, a PDF,
and optional LaTeX source.

00:42:21.640 --> 00:42:24.300
So already you should
be thinking, well,

00:42:24.300 --> 00:42:26.040
what does it mean
to train on arXiv?

00:42:26.040 --> 00:42:29.400
It's not clear,
because this is, well,

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:31.300
you have to convert
the PDF into text,

00:42:31.300 --> 00:42:36.560
or you can use the LaTeX source,
which is a bunch of files.

00:42:36.560 --> 00:42:39.800
It's not peer-reviewed, but
there is some approval process,

00:42:39.800 --> 00:42:44.440
and authors can choose
to maintain the rights,

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:47.720
or put in the Creative
Commons, which--

00:42:47.720 --> 00:42:51.440
so everything is actually
very clearly licensed.

00:42:51.440 --> 00:42:53.660
All the metadata is
under permissive license

00:42:53.660 --> 00:42:56.240
so you can use that
regardless, and then you can go

00:42:56.240 --> 00:42:58.120
and you look at
all the papers that

00:42:58.120 --> 00:42:59.380
are Creative Commons license.

00:42:59.380 --> 00:43:01.480
You download those,
and you can use them.

00:43:01.480 --> 00:43:08.160
And I think most arXiv
papers are Creative Commons.

00:43:08.160 --> 00:43:10.460
And then again,
this is available.

00:43:10.460 --> 00:43:14.120
You don't crawl arXiv,
you just go down to bulk

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:18.640
download it from some website.

00:43:18.640 --> 00:43:22.500
So any questions
about sources of data?

00:43:22.500 --> 00:43:23.300
Yeah?

00:43:23.300 --> 00:43:26.600
Are there any restrictions
on usage of data,

00:43:26.600 --> 00:43:29.760
which is generated by models?

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:31.720
Are there restrictions on data?

00:43:31.720 --> 00:43:33.000
Generated by models.

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:34.140
Generated by models.

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:34.640
Yeah.

00:43:34.640 --> 00:43:36.400
I mean, [? synthetic ?]
[? data ?] models.

00:43:36.400 --> 00:43:37.760
Oh, I see.

00:43:37.760 --> 00:43:39.802
We'll talk about that
a little bit later.

00:43:39.802 --> 00:43:41.760
So the question is, what
about model-generated,

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:43.100
like synthetic data?

00:43:43.100 --> 00:43:45.680
Are you allowed to use it?

00:43:45.680 --> 00:43:49.340
The short answer
is probably yes.

00:43:49.340 --> 00:43:49.840
Yeah?

00:43:49.840 --> 00:43:52.760
When using crawlers
like [INAUDIBLE] Crawl

00:43:52.760 --> 00:43:57.770
and stuff like that, I guess--
because you mentioned earlier

00:43:57.770 --> 00:43:59.890
that there are
websites that have

00:43:59.890 --> 00:44:02.130
a lot of pirated books on it.

00:44:02.130 --> 00:44:06.050
How do you ensure that
those types of websites

00:44:06.050 --> 00:44:08.750
don't show up in your crawl?

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:09.250
Yeah.

00:44:09.250 --> 00:44:11.290
So the question
is, there's a lot

00:44:11.290 --> 00:44:12.710
of websites with pirated books.

00:44:12.710 --> 00:44:14.252
So if you're just
doing a web crawl,

00:44:14.252 --> 00:44:15.710
you can't look at
all the websites.

00:44:15.710 --> 00:44:18.010
So how do you make sure
that everything is kosher?

00:44:18.010 --> 00:44:20.930
And that's part of the
difficulty is, you can't.

00:44:20.930 --> 00:44:25.710
And so, there's-- most
likely in Common Crawl,

00:44:25.710 --> 00:44:30.090
there are copyrighted books
and content that you are not

00:44:30.090 --> 00:44:34.010
supposed to train
on, and you can--

00:44:34.010 --> 00:44:35.110
will appeal to fair use.

00:44:35.110 --> 00:44:36.527
Remember, everything
is copyright.

00:44:36.527 --> 00:44:41.890
Books are not remarkably
different from your website,

00:44:41.890 --> 00:44:44.670
for example. both
are copyrighted.

00:44:44.670 --> 00:44:48.680
Probably, a book
author has more of a--

00:44:48.680 --> 00:44:50.330
if they want to go
into court, they'll

00:44:50.330 --> 00:44:52.913
probably be able to protect that
better because it's published

00:44:52.913 --> 00:44:58.410
than your website, but still.

00:44:58.410 --> 00:45:00.710
And I'll talk a little
bit about later.

00:45:00.710 --> 00:45:02.610
If you really, really
want to be careful,

00:45:02.610 --> 00:45:07.290
you cam do something
I'll show later.

00:45:07.290 --> 00:45:10.690
So what I'm going to
do now is to now talk

00:45:10.690 --> 00:45:13.810
about the various
datasets that are actually

00:45:13.810 --> 00:45:16.690
used in building models,
starting all the way back

00:45:16.690 --> 00:45:17.830
to 2019.

00:45:17.830 --> 00:45:20.590
So we've talked about the origin
of data and source of data,

00:45:20.590 --> 00:45:26.810
so you have a feeling for just
the general data landscape.

00:45:26.810 --> 00:45:29.050
And so, let's start with BERT.

00:45:29.050 --> 00:45:34.730
So BERT was from 2018, and
they trained-- back in the day,

00:45:34.730 --> 00:45:37.090
they just trained on
Wikipedia and books.

00:45:37.090 --> 00:45:39.450
So what is this books?

00:45:39.450 --> 00:45:41.750
So there's this website
called Smashwords.

00:45:41.750 --> 00:45:47.170
It was created that allow
anyone to just put an ebook up

00:45:47.170 --> 00:45:53.050
to publish, and they had about
half a million books in 2024.

00:45:53.050 --> 00:46:02.330
So in 2015, there was this paper
who just scraped Smashwords--

00:46:02.330 --> 00:46:08.770
sorry, took the books that were
free, and just made a corpus.

00:46:08.770 --> 00:46:10.290
This was back in
the innocent days

00:46:10.290 --> 00:46:12.390
when no one was paying
attention to any of this.

00:46:12.390 --> 00:46:15.530
So this books corpus was
around for many years

00:46:15.530 --> 00:46:20.890
in the academic community.

00:46:20.890 --> 00:46:22.890
Since then, it has
been taken down

00:46:22.890 --> 00:46:26.210
because it violated
the terms of service.

00:46:26.210 --> 00:46:29.970
Just because it was free and
you can get it, doesn't mean it

00:46:29.970 --> 00:46:33.890
was legally allowed.

00:46:33.890 --> 00:46:37.530
So one thing to
note about Bert is

00:46:37.530 --> 00:46:43.153
that the sequences were
documents rather than sentences.

00:46:43.153 --> 00:46:44.570
All the language
modeling research

00:46:44.570 --> 00:46:50.210
before that was
focused on sentences.

00:46:50.210 --> 00:46:54.000
So in 2019, GPT-2 came around.

00:46:54.000 --> 00:46:56.300
And so what did they do?

00:46:56.300 --> 00:47:02.020
So they wanted to get
high quality web content.

00:47:02.020 --> 00:47:05.620
And at that time, people
knew about Common Crawl,

00:47:05.620 --> 00:47:09.060
but somehow the Common
Crawl data was too messy.

00:47:09.060 --> 00:47:11.500
So what they did was came
up with this clever idea

00:47:11.500 --> 00:47:13.820
where you can take--

00:47:13.820 --> 00:47:17.900
you look at pages that are
outgoing links from Reddit posts

00:47:17.900 --> 00:47:20.580
with greater than three karma.

00:47:20.580 --> 00:47:24.640
So a good post must
link to good websites.

00:47:24.640 --> 00:47:30.940
So you grab those pages,
and you get a 40 gigabytes

00:47:30.940 --> 00:47:34.100
of text, 8 million pages.

00:47:34.100 --> 00:47:36.820
They never released
this data, but there

00:47:36.820 --> 00:47:39.620
was an open
replication of webtext,

00:47:39.620 --> 00:47:42.320
which people used quite a bit.

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:49.320
So that's GPT-2.

00:47:49.320 --> 00:47:54.700
So as you're going through
this, I guess think about both--

00:47:54.700 --> 00:47:58.000
there's many methods of
filtering and collecting data,

00:47:58.000 --> 00:48:03.480
and it's interesting to look
at these design choices, and.

00:48:03.480 --> 00:48:08.340
So CCNET was developed by,
at that time, Facebook.

00:48:08.340 --> 00:48:12.100
So the goal was to create
large high quality data

00:48:12.100 --> 00:48:13.360
sets for pre-training.

00:48:13.360 --> 00:48:14.860
And they were
especially interested

00:48:14.860 --> 00:48:18.660
in low resource languages.

00:48:18.660 --> 00:48:21.700
So they didn't want
some very manual process

00:48:21.700 --> 00:48:25.020
that only worked for English.

00:48:25.020 --> 00:48:26.840
So they did a bunch of things.

00:48:26.840 --> 00:48:32.340
They did deduplication,
and language identification

00:48:32.340 --> 00:48:36.740
to train a classifier
to detect a language,

00:48:36.740 --> 00:48:42.420
and keep only the language
that you're looking for.

00:48:42.420 --> 00:48:47.340
And for quality filtering,
the idea that they had was

00:48:47.340 --> 00:48:50.620
they wanted to keep documents
that look like Wikipedia.

00:48:50.620 --> 00:48:54.780
So Wikipedia at the time was
deemed good, high quality

00:48:54.780 --> 00:48:57.285
content, and they used
a language model that

00:48:57.285 --> 00:48:58.660
was trained on
Wikipedia, and you

00:48:58.660 --> 00:49:02.180
score a probability of a new
document under that language

00:49:02.180 --> 00:49:06.300
model to gauge how
Wikipedia-like it was.

00:49:06.300 --> 00:49:08.520
So rather than outgoing
links to Reddit,

00:49:08.520 --> 00:49:11.740
they use a language
model on Wikipedia.

00:49:11.740 --> 00:49:16.840
And they show that because
you can get much more data,

00:49:16.840 --> 00:49:22.340
you can actually outperform
just training on Wikipedia.

00:49:22.340 --> 00:49:25.300
And this was a tool
that will later

00:49:25.300 --> 00:49:29.020
appear in some other papers.

00:49:29.020 --> 00:49:32.880
So here is another work.

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:37.420
This is from Google in 2019.

00:49:37.420 --> 00:49:39.940
So this is called C4.

00:49:39.940 --> 00:49:45.780
So this paper as actually
more famous for the T5 model,

00:49:45.780 --> 00:49:47.670
which pushes the idea
that you should just

00:49:47.670 --> 00:49:52.030
treat all the NLP tasks in
a world as text-to-text.

00:49:52.030 --> 00:49:54.250
But actually, a big
contribution of this paper,

00:49:54.250 --> 00:49:58.030
which is a very long
report, was the C4 dataset.

00:49:58.030 --> 00:50:03.230
And the observation, which was
very clear at that time, which

00:50:03.230 --> 00:50:08.230
is that Common Crawl is mostly
not useful for natural language.

00:50:08.230 --> 00:50:12.030
There was this idea that you
have these small datasets,

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:14.510
and they were high
quality, or you had

00:50:14.510 --> 00:50:17.030
Common Crawl, which was a mess.

00:50:17.030 --> 00:50:19.390
And any attempt to train
on Common Crawl just led

00:50:19.390 --> 00:50:20.295
to junk results.

00:50:20.295 --> 00:50:21.670
So people were
thinking like, how

00:50:21.670 --> 00:50:26.110
do I get a larger but high
quality subset of Common Crawl?

00:50:26.110 --> 00:50:32.670
So we saw the Reddit idea, we
saw the Wikipedia-like language

00:50:32.670 --> 00:50:34.670
model idea.

00:50:34.670 --> 00:50:37.350
C4 used a different
idea, and this

00:50:37.350 --> 00:50:41.430
is saying, let's just define a
bunch of rules, which turned out

00:50:41.430 --> 00:50:42.890
to be fairly effective.

00:50:42.890 --> 00:50:46.950
So basically, they
keep lines that

00:50:46.950 --> 00:50:49.850
end in punctuation, that
have more than five words,

00:50:49.850 --> 00:50:53.150
remove pages with fewer
than three sentences,

00:50:53.150 --> 00:50:57.510
remove pages that
contain any bad words.

00:50:57.510 --> 00:51:02.550
Remove things like terms
of use, boilerplate.

00:51:02.550 --> 00:51:04.690
Interestingly, remove
the curly brace,

00:51:04.690 --> 00:51:08.930
which filters out a lot of
code-- so clearly at that time,

00:51:08.930 --> 00:51:11.830
they weren't thinking
about code models--

00:51:11.830 --> 00:51:17.270
and they filtered out
anything that was not English.

00:51:17.270 --> 00:51:21.730
The end result is that you
have 156 billion tokens,

00:51:21.730 --> 00:51:25.090
so 800 gigabytes of text.

00:51:25.090 --> 00:51:27.910
So this is much larger than
the GPT-2 dataset, which

00:51:27.910 --> 00:51:32.710
was only 40 gigabytes of text.

00:51:32.710 --> 00:51:35.630
A bit later there was
some analysis done in C4

00:51:35.630 --> 00:51:36.870
which showed--

00:51:36.870 --> 00:51:39.170
here's the websites
that were represented.

00:51:39.170 --> 00:51:41.670
So you have a
bunch of Wikipedia,

00:51:41.670 --> 00:51:46.800
patents was a very common thing.

00:51:46.800 --> 00:51:49.340
They also released a
WebText-like document.

00:51:49.340 --> 00:51:53.200
They also use this idea
of links in Reddit posts--

00:51:56.880 --> 00:51:59.920
from Reddit posts with
greater than three karma,

00:51:59.920 --> 00:52:04.160
and filtered to those pages.

00:52:04.160 --> 00:52:08.520
So they were able to
get, using this method,

00:52:08.520 --> 00:52:14.280
17gb of text using 12
Common Crawl dumps.

00:52:14.280 --> 00:52:16.960
Remember, webtext
was 40 gigabytes,

00:52:16.960 --> 00:52:22.960
which means that Common Crawl
is maybe incomplete, or doesn't

00:52:22.960 --> 00:52:25.160
have everything, because
if it had everything,

00:52:25.160 --> 00:52:28.320
you should be able to hit 40.

00:52:28.320 --> 00:52:30.380
And they showed
that at that time,

00:52:30.380 --> 00:52:31.960
you could use this
data to improve

00:52:31.960 --> 00:52:36.480
on a bunch of NLP benchmarks.

00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:40.360
So we've seen
using rules, things

00:52:40.360 --> 00:52:44.000
that look like Wikipedia,
Reddit links, and now let's

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:46.240
talk about GPT-3.

00:52:46.240 --> 00:52:50.280
So GPT-3, the dataset was--

00:52:50.280 --> 00:52:53.280
they used Common Crawl, they
did their own processing.

00:52:53.280 --> 00:52:57.160
There is this WebText, which was
essentially the GPT-2 dataset,

00:52:57.160 --> 00:52:59.160
but expanded.

00:52:59.160 --> 00:53:00.580
Notice that there's
some overlap.

00:53:00.580 --> 00:53:02.600
Common Crawl does
overlap with this,

00:53:02.600 --> 00:53:08.120
but this is, in some sense,
a more targeted distribution.

00:53:08.120 --> 00:53:10.560
There is this--
in the paper, it's

00:53:10.560 --> 00:53:16.400
described as Books1 and Books2,
which is internet-based books

00:53:16.400 --> 00:53:21.840
corpora, so this remains a
mystery exactly what it is,

00:53:21.840 --> 00:53:24.560
and Wikipedia.

00:53:24.560 --> 00:53:30.460
So the result was
about 500 gigabytes

00:53:30.460 --> 00:53:34.720
of text, 400 billion tokens.

00:53:34.720 --> 00:53:38.200
And to do the Common
Crawl processing,

00:53:38.200 --> 00:53:41.640
they trained a quality
classifier to distinguish things

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:45.400
they deemed to be high
quality from the rest,

00:53:45.400 --> 00:53:47.840
and they did some
fuzzy deduplication

00:53:47.840 --> 00:53:54.720
because WebText and
Common Crawl had dupes.

00:53:54.720 --> 00:54:01.360
So this paper followed the
idea of using a classifier

00:54:01.360 --> 00:54:05.720
to do quality classification.

00:54:05.720 --> 00:54:09.060
So after GPT-3 came out, this
was, I think, a big event.

00:54:09.060 --> 00:54:10.800
And there were a
number of efforts

00:54:10.800 --> 00:54:12.500
to try to do things in the open.

00:54:12.500 --> 00:54:14.960
So one of the
initial efforts was

00:54:14.960 --> 00:54:23.080
The Pile, which is a grassroots
effort from EleutherAI, where

00:54:23.080 --> 00:54:29.800
they had a bunch of people
in this Discord just jamming

00:54:29.800 --> 00:54:31.700
and figuring out
high quality sources.

00:54:31.700 --> 00:54:34.480
And they came up with
this list in the end.

00:54:34.480 --> 00:54:38.140
And today, this is still
pretty interesting and diverse.

00:54:38.140 --> 00:54:43.170
It has Common Crawl,
it has PubMed.

00:54:43.170 --> 00:54:47.130
This has this thing called
Books3, which we'll talk about.

00:54:47.130 --> 00:54:51.450
ArXiv, GitHub, which
we talked about.

00:54:51.450 --> 00:54:56.770
Wikipedia, IRC, another
BooksCorpus, Philosophy Papers,

00:54:56.770 --> 00:54:57.670
and so on.

00:55:04.090 --> 00:55:06.990
They included this
Enron Emails dataset.

00:55:06.990 --> 00:55:13.690
So Enron went bust in 2002 and
all the emails were released,

00:55:13.690 --> 00:55:16.850
and this is one of the few
email datasets we have,

00:55:16.850 --> 00:55:20.070
which is a weird distribution,
I think, for email.

00:55:20.070 --> 00:55:23.050
But that's what you get.

00:55:23.050 --> 00:55:25.170
So Project Gutenberg was--

00:55:25.170 --> 00:55:28.490
so let's talk about
books a little bit.

00:55:28.490 --> 00:55:34.730
So project Gutenberg
was started in 1971,

00:55:34.730 --> 00:55:39.890
and it only includes books that
received copyright clearance.

00:55:39.890 --> 00:55:42.030
So mostly books in
the public domain.

00:55:45.210 --> 00:55:48.310
There's this packaging
of Gutenberg into PG-19,

00:55:48.310 --> 00:55:54.290
which is Gutenberg before 2019.

00:55:54.290 --> 00:55:57.250
Which is, I guess most
of them, because for them

00:55:57.250 --> 00:56:01.610
to be in public domain,
you have to wait 75 years.

00:56:01.610 --> 00:56:08.970
So Books3, which is this
interesting dataset that

00:56:08.970 --> 00:56:11.090
appeared in The Pile.

00:56:11.090 --> 00:56:18.610
So this is described actually as
200k books from a shadow library

00:56:18.610 --> 00:56:23.730
called Bibliotik that concludes
all books from your favorite

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:25.530
authors.

00:56:25.530 --> 00:56:29.170
And at that time-- again, 2020,
no one was paying attention.

00:56:29.170 --> 00:56:35.930
People used it to train
models, and since then, it

00:56:35.930 --> 00:56:37.230
has been taken down.

00:56:37.230 --> 00:56:42.970
So you cannot use, or should
not use Books3 anymore.

00:56:42.970 --> 00:56:45.370
We'll come back to Books3 again.

00:56:45.370 --> 00:56:53.150
So Stack Exchange you've
probably used quite a bit,

00:56:53.150 --> 00:56:59.125
although I guess these days
maybe less so because of AI.

00:56:59.125 --> 00:57:01.000
There's a bunch of
user-contributed questions

00:57:01.000 --> 00:57:03.570
and answers, started in 2008.

00:57:03.570 --> 00:57:07.770
And one thing that is
interesting about this dataset

00:57:07.770 --> 00:57:12.610
is that it's a Q&amp;A
format, and this is quite

00:57:12.610 --> 00:57:16.850
close to a real application.

00:57:16.850 --> 00:57:20.090
So normally, you think about
pre-training data as here's

00:57:20.090 --> 00:57:23.250
Wikipedia articles,
just like raw text.

00:57:23.250 --> 00:57:26.250
But some parts of the
web are actually--

00:57:26.250 --> 00:57:29.650
look supervised, like
what you would want

00:57:29.650 --> 00:57:31.610
to ask your language model.

00:57:31.610 --> 00:57:37.140
Which I think, maybe,
helps the model

00:57:37.140 --> 00:57:40.740
learn certain types of
question answering behaviors.

00:57:40.740 --> 00:57:43.320
So not everything is
super magically emergent.

00:57:43.320 --> 00:57:46.120
It's like, well, this type of
data does exist on the web.

00:57:48.860 --> 00:57:52.880
So there's also-- this
data has metadata,

00:57:52.880 --> 00:57:54.340
which is like the
number of votes

00:57:54.340 --> 00:57:56.380
which are useful for filtering.

00:57:56.380 --> 00:58:02.120
And again, this data is
released in data dumps

00:58:02.120 --> 00:58:05.900
that allows you to just
download without crawling it.

00:58:05.900 --> 00:58:08.860
So now we're in 2021.

00:58:08.860 --> 00:58:11.700
There was another
paper from DeepMind.

00:58:11.700 --> 00:58:13.240
They trained a
model called Gopher,

00:58:13.240 --> 00:58:17.100
which was never
released, and actually

00:58:17.100 --> 00:58:20.932
was subsumed by Chinchilla.

00:58:20.932 --> 00:58:22.640
But the description
of the data, I think,

00:58:22.640 --> 00:58:23.560
is actually really good.

00:58:23.560 --> 00:58:24.620
You should read this
paper, because I

00:58:24.620 --> 00:58:26.500
think it's very
thorough in the way

00:58:26.500 --> 00:58:29.660
that they describe
the data processing.

00:58:29.660 --> 00:58:31.900
Well, except for the parts
where they don't tell you

00:58:31.900 --> 00:58:33.860
what's in the data.

00:58:33.860 --> 00:58:38.660
So they created
this massive web,

00:58:38.660 --> 00:58:42.260
and they had C4, which
remember, was the dataset

00:58:42.260 --> 00:58:44.700
that we looked at
before from Google.

00:58:44.700 --> 00:58:46.840
They train on books, news,
GitHub, and Wikipedia.

00:58:46.840 --> 00:58:50.860
They don't say anything
about how they got that.

00:58:50.860 --> 00:58:57.940
So for the MassiveWeb,
they kept English, deduped,

00:58:57.940 --> 00:59:03.260
and their quality filtering
was also based on manual rules.

00:59:03.260 --> 00:59:07.260
And part of the reason
for this is that they

00:59:07.260 --> 00:59:08.760
had more control over that.

00:59:08.760 --> 00:59:12.460
So I think, as
you can see, there

00:59:12.460 --> 00:59:20.860
is this division between
people who wanted to use rules,

00:59:20.860 --> 00:59:25.580
and people who wanted
to use classifiers.

00:59:25.580 --> 00:59:29.120
So they got, as
the name suggests,

00:59:29.120 --> 00:59:33.180
a massive amount of
text, but their model

00:59:33.180 --> 00:59:37.630
was only trained on a very
small fraction of this dataset.

00:59:40.630 --> 00:59:48.870
So in 2022 Llama 1 came out,
and the dataset here-- this

00:59:48.870 --> 00:59:54.150
was actually a very nice paper
in that it detailed their data

00:59:54.150 --> 00:59:54.970
processing.

00:59:54.970 --> 00:59:59.070
So this is probably one
of the last non-open,

00:59:59.070 --> 01:00:01.750
fully open models that
actually talk about their data

01:00:01.750 --> 01:00:02.870
processing.

01:00:02.870 --> 01:00:05.970
So they have Common Crawl
processed with CCNET,

01:00:05.970 --> 01:00:09.910
which you know about.

01:00:09.910 --> 01:00:14.230
The classification, though,
was whether the page

01:00:14.230 --> 01:00:16.990
was a reference of Wikipedia,
not whether it is actually

01:00:16.990 --> 01:00:19.830
a Wikipedia article.

01:00:19.830 --> 01:00:22.550
So the idea is that well,
maybe Wikipedia articles

01:00:22.550 --> 01:00:24.950
are too stylized.

01:00:24.950 --> 01:00:26.990
And we know that Wikipedia
articles references

01:00:26.990 --> 01:00:29.250
a bunch of other articles
which are presumably good,

01:00:29.250 --> 01:00:32.990
so let's call those
the good websites.

01:00:32.990 --> 01:00:37.870
They had C4, they did
a bunch of processing

01:00:37.870 --> 01:00:42.670
on GitHub to keep permissive
licenses, Wikipedia.

01:00:42.670 --> 01:00:47.910
They trained on Books3
and project Gutenberg.

01:00:47.910 --> 01:00:50.950
So Books3 really got
them in a lot of trouble

01:00:50.950 --> 01:00:53.030
because they're just
announcing to the world that

01:00:53.030 --> 01:00:54.450
hey, I trained on this dataset.

01:00:54.450 --> 01:00:55.908
And if you look
back, oh, this came

01:00:55.908 --> 01:00:57.158
from-- where did it come from?

01:00:57.158 --> 01:00:58.130
It came from The Pile.

01:00:58.130 --> 01:01:00.010
Oh, it came from
a shadow library.

01:01:00.010 --> 01:01:04.430
So that's why people don't
want to talk about their data

01:01:04.430 --> 01:01:06.510
anymore.

01:01:06.510 --> 01:01:11.750
So they looked at
arXiv, and actually used

01:01:11.750 --> 01:01:15.490
the LaTeX to do the
processing, Stack Exchange,

01:01:15.490 --> 01:01:17.740
and they got 1.2
trillion tokens.

01:01:17.740 --> 01:01:19.490
They didn't actually
release this dataset,

01:01:19.490 --> 01:01:22.870
but they had enough of a
description in their processing

01:01:22.870 --> 01:01:27.910
that this was reproduced
by Together's RedPajama V1

01:01:27.910 --> 01:01:33.030
dataset, which was then
used in other sources.

01:01:33.030 --> 01:01:36.230
So as you can see
here, RedPajama V1

01:01:36.230 --> 01:01:43.790
initially also contained Books3,
which has now been stripped out.

01:01:43.790 --> 01:01:48.110
So various decisions made early
on about copyright actually

01:01:48.110 --> 01:01:53.350
influence-- have a
fairly big watershed.

01:01:53.350 --> 01:01:55.890
So let's talk about RefinedWeb.

01:01:55.890 --> 01:02:03.630
So this is a paper that tried
to make a point that web data is

01:02:03.630 --> 01:02:05.130
all you need.

01:02:05.130 --> 01:02:07.593
So the web, in some
sense, is everything.

01:02:07.593 --> 01:02:09.510
And then we talked about
all these specialized

01:02:09.510 --> 01:02:14.230
sources, like there's GitHub
and arXiv, and Stack Exchange.

01:02:14.230 --> 01:02:16.030
And they said,
well, what happens

01:02:16.030 --> 01:02:19.710
if we just stick with the web?

01:02:19.710 --> 01:02:23.630
So they did a good job of doing
the transformation from HTML

01:02:23.630 --> 01:02:24.750
to text.

01:02:24.750 --> 01:02:30.320
They filtered using the Gopher
rules, which is basically keep

01:02:30.320 --> 01:02:32.600
things that look like English.

01:02:32.600 --> 01:02:34.480
They explicitly,
at that time, still

01:02:34.480 --> 01:02:38.600
said avoid ML-based
filtering to avoid biases.

01:02:38.600 --> 01:02:43.640
I don't want to find an overly
narrow subset of the web.

01:02:43.640 --> 01:02:47.700
They did some deduplication, and
they had five trillion tokens.

01:02:47.700 --> 01:02:51.960
They released about
600 billion of them.

01:02:51.960 --> 01:02:58.900
So FineWeb from Hugging Face
was a replication of RefinedWeb,

01:02:58.900 --> 01:03:00.180
but improved it.

01:03:00.180 --> 01:03:03.080
So they took all the Common
Crawl dumps at that time,

01:03:03.080 --> 01:03:05.240
did some filtering--

01:03:05.240 --> 01:03:09.960
again, using manual rules
to not inject biases--

01:03:09.960 --> 01:03:16.060
deduped, did PII removal,
and got 15 trillion tokens.

01:03:16.060 --> 01:03:19.520
So you can see that the
size of these datasets

01:03:19.520 --> 01:03:22.020
is growing quite a bit.

01:03:26.840 --> 01:03:31.320
So then there is this
Dolma dataset from AI2,

01:03:31.320 --> 01:03:36.560
and this is a dataset that
includes their own processing

01:03:36.560 --> 01:03:39.560
of Common Crawl, The Stack,
which we'll talk about,

01:03:39.560 --> 01:03:44.640
before C4, which you know about,
and a bunch of other things.

01:03:44.640 --> 01:03:53.200
And the Reddit dataset was from
this project called PushShift.

01:03:53.200 --> 01:03:55.760
And at that time, you
could still get this data,

01:03:55.760 --> 01:04:01.360
and you can train on it
before things got locked down.

01:04:01.360 --> 01:04:06.480
AI2 has their own crawl
of academic papers called

01:04:06.480 --> 01:04:10.400
Semantic Scholar, so they
derived a dataset based on that.

01:04:10.400 --> 01:04:13.960
So let's look at their
Common Crawl processing.

01:04:13.960 --> 01:04:19.180
So they use language
identification.

01:04:19.180 --> 01:04:23.360
So this was model-based,
but quality filtering still

01:04:23.360 --> 01:04:27.200
avoid model-based filtering.

01:04:27.200 --> 01:04:34.280
And then they removed toxicity
using rules and a classifier.

01:04:34.280 --> 01:04:37.720
And they got three trillion
tokens out of that.

01:04:37.720 --> 01:04:40.360
So DCLM, I think,
was maybe a point

01:04:40.360 --> 01:04:45.160
where this idea of
model-based quality filtering

01:04:45.160 --> 01:04:51.040
started to really
become the norm here.

01:04:51.040 --> 01:04:54.280
So DataComp, the
initial motivation

01:04:54.280 --> 01:04:56.800
was to define some
sort of pipeline

01:04:56.800 --> 01:05:02.600
so that people can try different
data methods in a standard way,

01:05:02.600 --> 01:05:04.240
and show results.

01:05:04.240 --> 01:05:06.600
But I think the main
way that I think

01:05:06.600 --> 01:05:10.280
people use this is just using
their dataset that they released

01:05:10.280 --> 01:05:14.200
to train models and
do other things.

01:05:14.200 --> 01:05:19.660
So they processed Common
Crawl to produce DCLM-pool.

01:05:19.660 --> 01:05:21.238
So this is completely
unfiltered.

01:05:21.238 --> 01:05:23.280
And if you look at this
dataset, this is massive.

01:05:23.280 --> 01:05:27.210
240 trillion tokens.

01:05:27.210 --> 01:05:29.290
This is probably more
than the number of tokens

01:05:29.290 --> 01:05:31.190
that anyone really trains on.

01:05:31.190 --> 01:05:34.190
But a lot of this is
fairly low quality,

01:05:34.190 --> 01:05:39.190
so then they had a pipeline
where all that gets filtered.

01:05:39.190 --> 01:05:42.330
If you only keep
English, you have

01:05:42.330 --> 01:05:45.030
some of rules to
really narrow it down.

01:05:45.030 --> 01:05:49.130
You do dedupe, and then you
do this model-based filtering,

01:05:49.130 --> 01:05:52.630
and you end up with
something that's 1.4%.

01:05:52.630 --> 01:05:57.290
And this is the data set
that we'll see actually works

01:05:57.290 --> 01:05:58.570
pretty well.

01:05:58.570 --> 01:06:04.490
So the model-based filtering--
and this was strangely good.

01:06:04.490 --> 01:06:11.970
So to train a classifier,
they took OpenHermes,

01:06:11.970 --> 01:06:15.170
which is essentially
instruction data that

01:06:15.170 --> 01:06:19.470
was generated by
GPT-4, and ELI5,

01:06:19.470 --> 01:06:23.790
which is a subreddit with
various questions and answers.

01:06:23.790 --> 01:06:28.370
So this is-- let's see.

01:06:28.370 --> 01:06:32.110
These are the type of
questions that are in ELI5.

01:06:35.570 --> 01:06:39.330
So anyway, you get the idea.

01:06:39.330 --> 01:06:44.910
So it's kind of weird,
but somehow this works.

01:06:44.910 --> 01:06:47.410
So the negative examples are
anything from RefinedWeb, which

01:06:47.410 --> 01:06:52.010
is, remember, it's just a
very loosely filtered version

01:06:52.010 --> 01:06:52.510
of the web.

01:06:52.510 --> 01:06:56.570
It's basically the web, and
it got 3.8 trillion tokens.

01:06:56.570 --> 01:06:59.050
They train a fastText
classifier-- think about it just

01:06:59.050 --> 01:07:01.370
as a linear classifier.

01:07:01.370 --> 01:07:06.090
And then they show that this
magical classifier, quality

01:07:06.090 --> 01:07:14.410
classifier, outperforms a bunch
of other things that they tried.

01:07:14.410 --> 01:07:16.210
So, DCLM.

01:07:16.210 --> 01:07:20.230
This became, for a while, at
least in the open community,

01:07:20.230 --> 01:07:26.130
a bit of a gold standard
for quality filtering.

01:07:26.130 --> 01:07:28.270
Let's move on to Nemotron.

01:07:28.270 --> 01:07:31.690
So Nemotron-- this
is from NVIDIA--

01:07:31.690 --> 01:07:35.510
they said that well, DCLM
filters too aggressively.

01:07:35.510 --> 01:07:39.250
It's removing most of the data.

01:07:39.250 --> 01:07:43.410
And remember, they only got--

01:07:43.410 --> 01:07:48.090
how many-- 3.8 trillion tokens.

01:07:48.090 --> 01:07:50.170
So we need more tokens.

01:07:50.170 --> 01:07:51.410
So what do we do?

01:07:51.410 --> 01:07:59.290
We are going to do something
more, I guess, elaborate.

01:07:59.290 --> 01:08:01.130
We're going to
prompt our existing

01:08:01.130 --> 01:08:07.287
model to score FineWeb documents
based on educational value.

01:08:07.287 --> 01:08:09.370
So we're going to prompt
a language model and say,

01:08:09.370 --> 01:08:11.170
is this educational or not?

01:08:11.170 --> 01:08:14.410
Go add a bunch of labels,
train a fastText model.

01:08:14.410 --> 01:08:16.830
So that's one classifier.

01:08:16.830 --> 01:08:20.380
Another classifier is just
using the DCLM classifier.

01:08:20.380 --> 01:08:23.540
So we're going to use
those classifiers.

01:08:23.540 --> 01:08:26.479
We're also going to
use synthetic data.

01:08:26.479 --> 01:08:31.183
So this is probably one of the--

01:08:31.183 --> 01:08:33.100
I don't know if-- it's
probably not the first,

01:08:33.100 --> 01:08:36.779
but, one of the main
datasets that really

01:08:36.779 --> 01:08:41.380
lean into synthetic
data for pre-training.

01:08:41.380 --> 01:08:43.580
So they had, for
low quality data,

01:08:43.580 --> 01:08:45.819
as deemed by these
classifiers, you

01:08:45.819 --> 01:08:49.300
use a language model to
rephrase it to make it more--

01:08:49.300 --> 01:08:51.460
look like Wikipedia.

01:08:51.460 --> 01:08:54.420
For high quality data,
we use a language model

01:08:54.420 --> 01:08:56.439
to generate various tasks.

01:08:56.439 --> 01:08:58.518
For example, given
a Wikipedia article,

01:08:58.518 --> 01:09:00.060
I can generate
question answers, or I

01:09:00.060 --> 01:09:04.740
can generate like, please
summarize this document,

01:09:04.740 --> 01:09:08.060
extract key information
from it, and so on.

01:09:08.060 --> 01:09:14.140
So they resulted in a dataset
that was 6 trillion tokens.

01:09:14.140 --> 01:09:20.340
So this is substantially
larger than DCLM.

01:09:20.340 --> 01:09:25.899
And just for reference,
this is still kind of

01:09:25.899 --> 01:09:30.000
small by some consideration.

01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:32.899
So Llama 3 was trained
on 15 trillion tokens,

01:09:32.899 --> 01:09:35.340
Qwen3 was trained
on 36 trillion.

01:09:35.340 --> 01:09:38.660
Although there is this--

01:09:38.660 --> 01:09:41.180
it's not clear how big
these unique tokens are,

01:09:41.180 --> 01:09:44.620
because when you look at
[INAUDIBLE] counts in language

01:09:44.620 --> 01:09:47.200
modeling papers, some
of it is repeated.

01:09:47.200 --> 01:09:49.420
Like if you do
two epochs, that's

01:09:49.420 --> 01:09:53.340
twice the number of tokens,
so you have to be careful

01:09:53.340 --> 01:09:55.740
when you look at those numbers.

01:09:55.740 --> 01:10:00.380
And they show that
the dataset is better.

01:10:00.380 --> 01:10:07.760
Their high quality subset
beats the previous datasets

01:10:07.760 --> 01:10:13.900
So up until now, we've seen
a bunch of different methods

01:10:13.900 --> 01:10:15.920
for filtering.

01:10:15.920 --> 01:10:20.810
I think all of these look
very similar at some level.

01:10:20.810 --> 01:10:23.190
You take a web crawl,
you either decide

01:10:23.190 --> 01:10:24.610
I'm going to use
rules to filter,

01:10:24.610 --> 01:10:25.690
or I'm going to use a model.

01:10:25.690 --> 01:10:27.148
If I'm going to
use a model, then I

01:10:27.148 --> 01:10:29.390
have to decide what
looks like good data,

01:10:29.390 --> 01:10:31.910
and then try to
train a classifier,

01:10:31.910 --> 01:10:35.130
and classify all my
documents, and select.

01:10:35.130 --> 01:10:36.710
And there seems
to be a trade-off

01:10:36.710 --> 01:10:39.170
between you can take a
lot of Common Crawl--

01:10:39.170 --> 01:10:44.650
you can get 240 trillion
tokens if you want,

01:10:44.650 --> 01:10:47.310
but that's probably going
to be really low quality.

01:10:47.310 --> 01:10:49.650
Or you can get one
trillion tokens,

01:10:49.650 --> 01:10:55.230
and there's some
sweet spot in-between.

01:10:55.230 --> 01:11:00.990
Let me talk about
two final things.

01:11:00.990 --> 01:11:05.990
So code, and going back to
this question of licensing.

01:11:05.990 --> 01:11:09.270
So The Stack is a
very nice project

01:11:09.270 --> 01:11:14.150
that was trying to make a really
good coding data set, since it

01:11:14.150 --> 01:11:17.150
was clear by 2022
that coding was

01:11:17.150 --> 01:11:19.550
going to be really important.

01:11:19.550 --> 01:11:21.910
So what they did in
the initial version

01:11:21.910 --> 01:11:27.310
is that they clone 137 repos.

01:11:27.310 --> 01:11:30.270
They kept the ones that
were permissively licensed,

01:11:30.270 --> 01:11:36.530
remove near-duplicates, and
resulted in one-- sorry,

01:11:36.530 --> 01:11:38.630
three terabytes of code.

01:11:38.630 --> 01:11:42.390
And then in 2024,
they had an update.

01:11:42.390 --> 01:11:46.490
And here they took
more the metadata,

01:11:46.490 --> 01:11:50.370
like the issues, comments,
and PRs from GitHub,

01:11:50.370 --> 01:11:52.630
the repositories from
the Software Heritage,

01:11:52.630 --> 01:11:54.230
which we talked about.

01:11:54.230 --> 01:11:57.750
They also scraped documentation
from various websites

01:11:57.750 --> 01:11:59.950
by crawling them.

01:11:59.950 --> 01:12:01.690
They did a bunch of processing.

01:12:01.690 --> 01:12:05.732
So in GitHub repos, you have
binary files which you probably

01:12:05.732 --> 01:12:06.690
don't want to train on.

01:12:06.690 --> 01:12:09.350
They have malware,
you get rid of that.

01:12:09.350 --> 01:12:12.770
A lot of GitHub, especially
these PRs, are bots,

01:12:12.770 --> 01:12:14.270
so you have to filter that.

01:12:14.270 --> 01:12:17.670
Dedupe, do PII redaction.

01:12:17.670 --> 01:12:20.810
There's a lot pull
requests and stuff,

01:12:20.810 --> 01:12:24.590
and they basically subsample
to keep it representative

01:12:24.590 --> 01:12:27.110
and the dataset manageable.

01:12:27.110 --> 01:12:30.730
They also did this thing,
which was I thought was nice,

01:12:30.730 --> 01:12:35.110
which is that there's many
programming languages out there.

01:12:35.110 --> 01:12:38.350
There's a lot of
Python, a lot of C,

01:12:38.350 --> 01:12:42.690
but low resource languages like
Nim-- which I hadn't even heard

01:12:42.690 --> 01:12:46.690
of-- they're not that common.

01:12:46.690 --> 01:12:50.190
So what they do is
they compile this code

01:12:50.190 --> 01:12:52.410
into a low-level
intermediate language,

01:12:52.410 --> 01:12:56.730
LLVM, which everything, like C
Compiler, can compile into this,

01:12:56.730 --> 01:12:59.510
and they have juxtaposed
the low resource

01:12:59.510 --> 01:13:01.910
language and the
intermediate representation.

01:13:01.910 --> 01:13:05.390
So then the language
model can actually

01:13:05.390 --> 01:13:10.290
learn the mapping between the
shared low level representation,

01:13:10.290 --> 01:13:13.400
which has a lot of data on,
and the thing that it does

01:13:13.400 --> 01:13:16.160
has less data on.

01:13:16.160 --> 01:13:21.880
And just for fun, you add
in all the other good stuff

01:13:21.880 --> 01:13:22.740
that you can.

01:13:22.740 --> 01:13:26.200
So Stack V2 is
mostly code, and then

01:13:26.200 --> 01:13:32.960
plus other things that were used
to train their coding models.

01:13:32.960 --> 01:13:36.240
And one thing
about pull requests

01:13:36.240 --> 01:13:42.100
is that pull requests and
all the metadata is not,

01:13:42.100 --> 01:13:44.660
by its nature, a
linearized sequence.

01:13:44.660 --> 01:13:48.520
So there has to be some
steps taken to linearize it.

01:13:48.520 --> 01:13:50.440
So one important thing
you have to decide

01:13:50.440 --> 01:13:52.660
is how much context to provide.

01:13:52.660 --> 01:13:55.240
So for example, an
event might just be,

01:13:55.240 --> 01:13:56.960
I change one line of code.

01:13:56.960 --> 01:13:59.440
And that could just be
one line, but presumably

01:13:59.440 --> 01:14:03.520
to learn that, you want to
provide some context, maybe

01:14:03.520 --> 01:14:06.360
a few lines around it, or maybe
the entire file surrounding

01:14:06.360 --> 01:14:07.120
that diff.

01:14:07.120 --> 01:14:08.600
And these are some
design decisions

01:14:08.600 --> 01:14:12.960
that you have to make
for linearization.

01:14:12.960 --> 01:14:16.240
And so at the end of the day,
the tokens that they train on

01:14:16.240 --> 01:14:18.320
look something like
this, where it's

01:14:18.320 --> 01:14:23.920
this XML-like structured
data, where you have the PR,

01:14:23.920 --> 01:14:26.360
and then you have
a bunch of diffs.

01:14:26.360 --> 01:14:29.400
And then for comments, you
have basically the events

01:14:29.400 --> 01:14:35.600
of a comment was posted, what
the review state of that PR was

01:14:35.600 --> 01:14:37.200
and so on and so forth.

01:14:37.200 --> 01:14:39.640
So it's not just
learning how to generate

01:14:39.640 --> 01:14:44.800
code, but also the software
development process around code.

01:14:44.800 --> 01:14:49.760
So finally, I'll talk
about the common pile.

01:14:49.760 --> 01:14:52.640
So recall that almost all
the data on the internet

01:14:52.640 --> 01:14:55.160
is copyrighted.

01:14:55.160 --> 01:14:57.160
Some of it is permissively
licensed, some of it

01:14:57.160 --> 01:14:59.920
is even in the public domain.

01:14:59.920 --> 01:15:03.740
And while you can appeal
to fair use and say,

01:15:03.740 --> 01:15:08.920
I'm going to train on it anyway,
this is not quite settled.

01:15:08.920 --> 01:15:11.200
So if you're very,
very risk-averse,

01:15:11.200 --> 01:15:18.140
then you say, well, if I
don't know whether it is OK,

01:15:18.140 --> 01:15:19.200
that's a no.

01:15:19.200 --> 01:15:23.240
If you take that attitude, then
that's what common pile did.

01:15:23.240 --> 01:15:27.760
So can you train a good model
with only permissively licensed

01:15:27.760 --> 01:15:28.380
data?

01:15:30.960 --> 01:15:33.640
Or rather, how far can you get?

01:15:33.640 --> 01:15:41.600
So this was a project that
went and scoured the internet

01:15:41.600 --> 01:15:44.160
for all the different
types of data

01:15:44.160 --> 01:15:47.740
that they could possibly find
that was worth training on,

01:15:47.740 --> 01:15:49.520
that was permissively licensed.

01:15:49.520 --> 01:15:54.360
It includes Stack V2 in code.

01:15:54.360 --> 01:15:56.560
It turns out that a lot
of government proceedings

01:15:56.560 --> 01:16:01.040
are actually
permissively licensed,

01:16:01.040 --> 01:16:08.690
wikis, some things on the web--
some new sites are actually

01:16:08.690 --> 01:16:14.690
permissively licensed,
academic papers, online forums,

01:16:14.690 --> 01:16:18.670
things that are in the public
domain, educational resources,

01:16:18.670 --> 01:16:20.010
and so on.

01:16:20.010 --> 01:16:22.210
So in the end, there
were 8 terabytes

01:16:22.210 --> 01:16:24.810
of data, which is
actually pretty

01:16:24.810 --> 01:16:29.450
good for permissively
licensed data.

01:16:29.450 --> 01:16:32.970
Doing this project is
actually much harder

01:16:32.970 --> 01:16:35.565
than maybe it sounds
at first glance.

01:16:35.565 --> 01:16:37.690
It's not just as simple
oh, you look at the license

01:16:37.690 --> 01:16:42.490
and it's like, oh yep,
Apache, or CC, good.

01:16:42.490 --> 01:16:48.050
There's some subtleties,
versus license laundering.

01:16:48.050 --> 01:16:51.350
So people are sloppy
with licenses.

01:16:51.350 --> 01:16:53.490
So people might take
some copyright work

01:16:53.490 --> 01:16:56.730
and just slap a CC BY on it.

01:16:56.730 --> 01:16:58.430
Anyone can write
this on the internet,

01:16:58.430 --> 01:17:06.370
and it's hard to tell
whether this is real or not.

01:17:06.370 --> 01:17:10.150
There's also this common
practice where, for example,

01:17:10.150 --> 01:17:14.510
Dolma is permissively licensed.

01:17:14.510 --> 01:17:17.730
Remember, Dolma is
the AI2 collection.

01:17:17.730 --> 01:17:22.250
But collection licenses don't
extend to the individual works.

01:17:22.250 --> 01:17:26.103
So the collection is--

01:17:26.103 --> 01:17:27.770
I mean, first of all,
there's a question

01:17:27.770 --> 01:17:28.885
of whether collections--

01:17:28.885 --> 01:17:30.510
I think collections
can be copyrighted,

01:17:30.510 --> 01:17:34.650
but I guess you can put
a license on anything.

01:17:34.650 --> 01:17:41.270
But the individual works
are not necessarily good.

01:17:41.270 --> 01:17:43.810
So you can't just look at
data sets on Hugging Face,

01:17:43.810 --> 01:17:46.018
and you-- many data sets
on Hugging Face you see,

01:17:46.018 --> 01:17:47.310
they have a permissive license.

01:17:47.310 --> 01:17:49.170
But if you dig
deeper, it's actually

01:17:49.170 --> 01:17:52.850
not permissively licensed
at the individual level.

01:17:52.850 --> 01:17:56.210
And they also made a
decision to forego training

01:17:56.210 --> 01:18:00.570
on any synthetic data, because
training language models

01:18:00.570 --> 01:18:04.690
on unlicensed data is unclear.

01:18:04.690 --> 01:18:12.740
So probably it's fine
because technically, these

01:18:12.740 --> 01:18:15.460
are open weight models
with MIT license.

01:18:15.460 --> 01:18:19.340
You can do whatever.

01:18:19.340 --> 01:18:21.040
You can use them
however you want,

01:18:21.040 --> 01:18:25.900
but these language models
were presumably also trained

01:18:25.900 --> 01:18:27.120
on unlicensed data.

01:18:27.120 --> 01:18:29.900
So it's a little bit
of data laundering

01:18:29.900 --> 01:18:33.040
if you are really honest here.

01:18:36.100 --> 01:18:39.780
So with this data,
they compare it

01:18:39.780 --> 01:18:46.980
with a bunch of other models
like the first Llama, MPT,

01:18:46.980 --> 01:18:48.180
and Qwen.

01:18:48.180 --> 01:18:52.800
And on a bunch of benchmarks,
they show that this is not bad.

01:18:52.800 --> 01:18:58.360
I would say it's not as good
as the Qwen models for sure,

01:18:58.360 --> 01:19:04.670
but it's certainly
outperforming the very old--

01:19:04.670 --> 01:19:09.100
this is from 2023, I guess.

01:19:09.100 --> 01:19:11.460
So quite old models.

01:19:11.460 --> 01:19:13.700
So I would say,
the conclusion here

01:19:13.700 --> 01:19:16.740
is that you can do
reasonably, but I

01:19:16.740 --> 01:19:19.060
think it's still
pretty tough to compete

01:19:19.060 --> 01:19:21.400
without doing more tokens.

01:19:21.400 --> 01:19:23.600
But I don't think this
is the final word on it,

01:19:23.600 --> 01:19:26.220
and I think you can probably
eke more out of publicly

01:19:26.220 --> 01:19:29.900
permissive licenses if you try.

01:19:29.900 --> 01:19:34.100
OK, so to summarize
this lecture here.

01:19:34.100 --> 01:19:38.640
So maybe hopefully now I've
given you some appreciation

01:19:38.640 --> 01:19:40.800
that data is a very rich topic.

01:19:40.800 --> 01:19:42.980
It's not that data just
falls from the sky,

01:19:42.980 --> 01:19:47.740
or you just go on Hugging Face
and you download a dataset.

01:19:47.740 --> 01:19:49.920
Data has to come from somewhere.

01:19:49.920 --> 01:19:53.460
And there's also a technical
but also social context

01:19:53.460 --> 01:19:59.540
in which data comes,
because at the top,

01:19:59.540 --> 01:20:03.420
the internet is a
bunch of live services.

01:20:03.420 --> 01:20:06.142
Someone has to do the work
of producing raw data,

01:20:06.142 --> 01:20:08.100
whether it be the website
that gives you dumps,

01:20:08.100 --> 01:20:11.580
or someone has to build
a crawler, or something.

01:20:11.580 --> 01:20:14.860
And then, someone has to make
the decision of how to process

01:20:14.860 --> 01:20:17.580
it into usable form--

01:20:17.580 --> 01:20:21.100
filtering, transformation,
deduplication, all these things

01:20:21.100 --> 01:20:26.420
have an impact on the quality
of your final language model.

01:20:26.420 --> 01:20:28.580
And notice that
filtering is probably one

01:20:28.580 --> 01:20:31.000
of the most important things.

01:20:31.000 --> 01:20:33.940
How do you go from
200 trillion tokens

01:20:33.940 --> 01:20:38.080
to less than three
trillion tokens?

01:20:38.080 --> 01:20:42.340
I think that obviously is a
huge reduction, which I think

01:20:42.340 --> 01:20:45.420
merits a lot of attention.

01:20:45.420 --> 01:20:48.500
Data is, in some sense,
a key ingredient that

01:20:48.500 --> 01:20:50.280
differentiates language models.

01:20:50.280 --> 01:20:53.700
A lot of language models
are roughly the same kind

01:20:53.700 --> 01:20:58.420
of transformer architecture,
but data is depending

01:20:58.420 --> 01:21:00.600
on how you process it.

01:21:00.600 --> 01:21:03.470
It can make a pretty
big difference.

01:21:03.470 --> 01:21:05.310
There's plenty of legal
and ethical issues

01:21:05.310 --> 01:21:10.070
around data, more than I
can get into this lecture.

01:21:10.070 --> 01:21:15.270
And also, this
process is very messy.

01:21:15.270 --> 01:21:19.110
Unlike some of the other parts
of this class where things

01:21:19.110 --> 01:21:24.310
are maybe more based
on first principles,

01:21:24.310 --> 01:21:28.110
data processing is, right
now at least, a lot just

01:21:28.110 --> 01:21:30.150
based on vibes you set up.

01:21:30.150 --> 01:21:32.530
You define this classifier,
you define this rule,

01:21:32.530 --> 01:21:34.390
you set some threshold.

01:21:34.390 --> 01:21:36.250
So there are many
opportunities to improve.

01:21:36.250 --> 01:21:38.510
So as you do your
assignment four,

01:21:38.510 --> 01:21:41.450
maybe think about whether
there's better ways to do this,

01:21:41.450 --> 01:21:45.150
and this could be maybe
a research direction.

01:21:45.150 --> 01:21:47.090
OK, so that's it for today.

01:21:47.090 --> 01:21:49.290
So next time I'll continue
talking about data.

01:21:49.290 --> 01:21:52.030
I'll talk a bit about
post-training data,

01:21:52.030 --> 01:21:56.080
and a bit more about filtering.

